---
<p>The shop from <ArticleRef slug="caching-patterns">Caching patterns</ArticleRef> has done everything that article recommended. When the merchant cuts the kettle from £39 to £35, the application commits the new price to the <Term kind="database">database</Term> first and then deletes <code>product:42</code> from <Term kind="redis">Redis</Term>. The next read misses, loads £35 and caches it. Yet one afternoon a customer complains that the product page showed £39 for most of an hour after the price had changed. The logs show the update committing and the delete succeeding. Nothing failed.</p>

<p>Nothing needed to fail. A cache is a copy, and a copy can only follow the original by being told about each change, through messages that travel separately from the change itself. Those messages can arrive late, be lost, or arrive in the wrong order relative to other requests filling the cache at the same moment. Keeping cached data close to the database despite that is called <strong>cache invalidation</strong>, and its failures are quiet: every request succeeds, and some of them return the wrong answer.</p>

<p>This article first pins down what a cache can promise. Then it takes the classic races one at a time, works out how often they happen, and shows the remedies that shrink or close them: a short TTL (time to live), deleting twice, leases and versioned values. After that come lost deletes, one change that affects many cached results, and the person who expects to see their own change at once. By the end you will be able to say what consistency your cache provides, recognise each race in a design review, and choose the cheapest remedy that is good enough for each kind of data.</p>

<h2 id="promises">What a cache can promise</h2>

<p>Start with the case where everything works. The writer commits £35, and a moment later its <code>DEL</code> reaches Redis. In between, Redis still holds £39, and a read in that gap is a <Term kind="stale" mark>stale</Term> read: a <Term kind="hit" mark>hit</Term> that returns an old value. The gap is usually a millisecond or two, but never zero, because the commit and the delete go to two separate systems.</p>

<p>Figure 1 stretches that gap so you can see it. The top band is the price in the database, the middle band is the copy in Redis, and the bottom row shows reads as they arrive. Each <Term kind="write" mark>write</Term> appears as a square on the database band, and its delete as a square on the Redis band when it lands.</p>

<WindowFigure number={1} />

<p>Watch a write land. The database band moves to the new price at once; the Redis band keeps the old price, shaded as stale, until the delete arrives, and reads in between are stale hits. Then the copy disappears, the next read is a <Term kind="miss" mark>miss</Term>, and the copy comes back with the new price. Drag the slider and the stale stretch grows and shrinks with it: the staleness after a write is exactly how long the delete takes to arrive.</p>

<p>Now set Deletes to "Are lost". Each change still commits, but its delete never reaches Redis, as happens when the writer crashes between its two steps or its request to Redis times out. After the next change the copy stays at the old price, and every read is stale, until the copy <Term kind="expiry" mark>expires</Term>: watch for the expiry mark on the Redis band. However long deletes stay lost, no stale stretch outlasts the copy's 15-second lifetime. In production a single lost delete is often cleared sooner, by the next change's delete; the TTL is what ends it when no other change comes.</p>

<p>Those two observations are the first two of four promises a cache can make:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Bounded staleness</strong>: a read never returns a value more than <em>B</em> seconds out of date. When invalidation works, staleness is the time the delete takes to arrive. When an invalidation is lost, or a race stores an old value, the bound is the TTL plus however old the value already was when it was stored: usually the time the fill took, or a replica's lag.</li>
  <li><strong>Eventual convergence</strong>: if writes stop, the cache ends up agreeing with the database. Cache-aside with a TTL on every key guarantees this, because every copy eventually expires and is reloaded.</li>
  <li><strong>Read-your-writes</strong>: after a write returns, the same user's next read sees it, or something newer. This is one of the session guarantees Douglas Terry and colleagues named in 1994 for weakly consistent replicated data. It is a promise to one person, and the one users notice most when it breaks.</li>
  <li><strong>Monotonic reads</strong>, another of Terry's guarantees: once a user has seen a value, they never later see an older one. A customer who has seen £35 must not see £39 on the next page. A cache breaks this when one request hits a fresh copy and the next misses and reads a lagging replica, or when an old value is stored after a fresh one was read.</li>
</ul>

<p>Notice what is missing: a cache-aside cache cannot promise that every read sees the latest committed write, because there is always a window between the commit and the delete. If a value must never be stale, as with the price you charge a customer, read it from the database. The rest of this article is about keeping the window short, keeping it from staying open, and keeping it closed for the person who is looking.</p>

<p>Meta measures its caches in these terms: the fraction of cache writes that agree with the database within a given time. For TAO, its cache of the social graph, it reports improving from six nines (99.9999%) to ten nines of writes consistent within five minutes. You can measure your own cache the same way: re-read a small sample of cache hits from the primary database, count mismatches older than your expected window, and alert on that rate. The question is not "is it consistent?" but "how stale, how often, and for how long?"</p>

<h2 id="stale-refill">The stale-set race</h2>

<p>So far the copy was correct when it was stored. The customer's hour of £39 came from a copy that was already wrong when it went in. Figure 2 shows how. Step through it with Next, and choose where the delay comes from with the control above it.</p>

<RaceFigure number={2} />

<p>Every participant did the right thing. The problem is that the reader's database read and its <code>SET</code>, together called a <strong>fill</strong>, are two steps with a gap between them, and the writer's delete landed in that gap. It arrived before there was anything to delete, so it removed nothing, and the reader then stored a value that was already out of date, with a full hour to live.</p>

<p>Here are the Redis commands in the order they arrived, with Redis's replies:</p>

<pre><code>{`# The reader misses, then reads £39 from the database.
GET product:42                          → (nil)
# The writer commits £35, then deletes. Nothing to delete yet.
DEL product:42                          → (integer) 0
# The reader resumes and stores what it read.
SET product:42 '{"price":39}' EX 3600   → OK
# Every later read is a hit, and stale, for an hour.
GET product:42                          → {"price":39}
TTL product:42                          → (integer) 3600`}</code></pre>

<p>Facebook's memcache paper calls this a <strong>stale set</strong>, and Meta's engineers describe the same sequence in their own words: a cache fill for an old value is in flight, the value changes, the invalidation arrives first, and the fill then lands.</p>

<p>Choose "a lagging replica" in Figure 2 to see the same race with nobody pausing at all. Many applications send reads to a database replica, which receives changes asynchronously and is often milliseconds behind the primary, and seconds under load. A reader that misses just after the delete reads the replica, gets the old row, and caches it. The replica catches up a moment later; the cache does not. With replicas, the window is the replication lag, and it is open after every write.</p>

<p>A third variant is the one <ArticleRef slug="caching-patterns">Caching patterns</ArticleRef> warned about: deleting before committing lets a reader miss in between and put the old row back. The fix there is the order: commit, then delete.</p>

<p>The remedy most people reach for first is the TTL. It does not prevent the race; it limits the damage. With <code>EX 60</code> instead of <code>EX 3600</code>, the trace above would have left £39 for a minute rather than an hour. Whether a minute is acceptable depends on how often this happens.</p>

<h2 id="how-often">How often it happens, and how long it lasts</h2>

<p>For the race to happen, a write must find a fill in progress: one that read the database before the commit and stores its value after the delete. Call the fill time δ (delta), and say fills start at a rate of <em>f</em> a second. By Little's law, the average number of fills in progress is <em>f</em> × δ, and when that is small it is also roughly the chance that a write finds one.</p>

<p>A key that is read often fills after every delete and every expiry, so <em>f</em> is roughly the write rate <em>w</em> plus one fill per TTL, <em>w</em> + 1/<em>T</em>. An old value stored by a race stays until the next write deletes it or it expires. If writes are much more frequent than expiries, <em>f</em> ≈ <em>w</em>, and:</p>

<p class="formula">races a second ≈ <em>w</em> × <em>f</em>δ ≈ <em>w</em> × <em>w</em>δ<br />each lasts until the next write ≈ 1/<em>w</em><br />share of time stale ≈ rate × duration ≈ <em>w</em> × δ</p>

<p>If expiries are more frequent, <em>f</em> ≈ 1/<em>T</em>, races happen about <em>w</em> × δ/<em>T</em> times a second, each lasts about <em>T</em>, and the product is again <em>w</em> × δ. Either way:</p>

<p class="formula">share of time a key holds an old value ≈ <em>w</em> × δ<br />(writes a second × mean fill time)</p>

<p>A product written once a minute with fills that take 50 ms on average should be stale from this race about 1/60 × 0.05 = 0.08% of the time. Figure 3 checks that against a simulation of fifty such keys for a day, grouping the stale stretches by length. A bar's height is the stale time in stretches of that length, so together the bars are the time spent stale; the number above a bar counts the stretches.</p>

<DayFigure number={3} />

<p>With the defaults, the fifty keys store an old value 73 times and spend about 55 minutes stale between them, out of 72,000 key-minutes: 0.077%, close to the prediction. Most stretches end at the key's next write, tens of seconds to a few minutes later. Now try each control on its own.</p>

<p>Write every 6 seconds and the races multiply about eighty-fold, because more writes each find more fills in progress, but the next write cuts each stretch short. The bars move left, and together they hold about ten times the stale time of the dashed outline of the defaults: the stale share rises tenfold, to about 0.75%. Every 10 minutes, one race is left in the day. Drag the fill time and the share grows in proportion: 200 ms gives about four times the share of 50 ms. That is why a slow database, a garbage-collection pause or a retry inside the fill makes this race more common.</p>

<p>Then set the TTL to 1 minute, and notice what does not change. There are more races, 104 instead of 73, because every expiry starts a fill that can race, but each is cut off within a minute: the bars move left and the counts rise, while the total stays the same. A short TTL caps the worst case; it does not reduce the average. It fixes a customer seeing £39 for an hour, not how often anyone sees a wrong price.</p>

<p>That tells you which keys need more than a TTL. For most data, written rarely and filled quickly, the race is a rounding error. It deserves attention for keys that are written often, filled slowly or read from replicas, and for values where even a short stale stretch is expensive. Remember too that the rate is per key. A shop with a hundred thousand products, each racing once a month, has about a hundred thousand stale prices a month, a few thousand every day.</p>

<p>The simulation is a model, not a measurement. It leaves out coalesced fills (which make races rarer), replicas (which make them far more common) and lost deletes. Against a real Redis, with a simulated database and a writer once a second, two to four fills a minute stored an old version: the race happens with real commands, while the figure's numbers come from the model.</p>

<h2 id="double-delete">Deleting twice: delayed double delete</h2>

<p>The race leaves an old value in Redis just after the writer's delete. An obvious response is to delete again a little later, after any fill in progress has finished. This is <strong>delayed double delete</strong>: delete after the commit, then again <em>D</em> milliseconds later. A racing fill that lands before the second delete is cleaned up; one that lands after it stays until it expires.</p>

<p>So everything depends on <em>D</em>. Figure 4 shows when racing fills store their old value, measured from the first delete. The marker is the second delete: bars to its left are removed, bars to its right stay.</p>

<DoubleDeleteFigure number={4} />

<p>Start with readers reading the primary and drag the delay. A short delay removes the fast fills, the tall bars near zero, but a surprisingly large share of the old values arrive much later. That is not because slow fills are common; in this model only one fill in fifty is held up. It is because a fill must be in progress when the write commits, and a fill that takes a second is in progress fifty times as often as one that takes 20 ms. The races are made mostly of slow fills, so the old values they store arrive late. Even a 2-second delay leaves about a fifth of them. How large that late share is depends on how heavy your own fill-time tail is, but the effect holds whatever its size: races favour slow fills.</p>

<p>Now switch to a replica 500 ms behind. Most writes now produce an old value (about two in three in this model, where the key is read twice a second, and nearly all for a busy key), because any read in the first half second reads the replica's old row. They all land within about 600 ms, so a 500 ms delay removes almost all of them and a 100 ms delay only a fifth. The delay must exceed the replication lag, and lag is not a constant: it grows exactly when the database is busy.</p>

<p>Delayed double delete has three more costs. Readers can see the old value for up to <em>D</em>. The second delete must survive the writer crashing during the delay, so it needs a durable queue or job, not a timer in the process. And it does nothing when the first delete never happens.</p>

<p>To close the window, the cache needs to know which fills started before the change. That is what a lease does.</p>

<h2 id="leases">Leases: letting the delete cancel the fill</h2>

<p>Facebook's memcache paper introduced <strong>leases</strong> to solve exactly this race. When a client misses, the server gives it a lease: a 64-bit token bound to that key, which the client presents when it stores the value. A delete of the key invalidates any outstanding token, so a fill that began before a change and arrives after its delete carries a token that no longer counts, and is refused. The paper compares it to load-link/store-conditional, the processor instructions that update memory only if nobody has written it in between.</p>

<p>Redis has no leases built in, but since Redis 8.4 it can do the same thing in one command per step, by keeping the lease in the key itself. On a miss, the reader writes a placeholder holding a token only it knows, and its fill stores the value only if the key still holds that placeholder. A writer's delete removes the placeholder, so a fill that started before the delete finds it gone. Step through Figure 5 with the lease and without it.</p>

<LeaseFigure number={5} />

<p>With the lease, the writer's <code>DEL</code> finally has something to delete. The reader's late fill asks Redis to store £39 only if the key equals <code>lease:r1</code>; it does not, so Redis replies nil and stores nothing. The next reader takes a fresh lease, reads £35 and fills normally.</p>

<p>In code, the read path gains one command on a miss:</p>

<pre><code>{`def get_product(product_id):
    key = f"product:{product_id}"
    cached = redis.get(key)
    if cached is not None and not cached.startswith(b"lease:"):
        return json.loads(cached)                  # a hit
    token = f"lease:{uuid4().hex}"
    # Take the lease before reading the database, with an expiry
    # a little longer than a slow fill:
    # SET product:42 lease:… NX PX 2000
    # If this reader crashes, every reader of the key goes to the
    # database until the lease expires.
    if not redis.set(key, token, nx=True, px=2_000):
        return db.fetch_product(product_id)        # another reader is filling
    product = db.fetch_product(product_id)
    # Fill only if our lease is still there (Redis 8.4 and later):
    # SET product:42 <json> IFEQ lease:… EX 300
    redis.execute_command("SET", key, json.dumps(product),
                          "IFEQ", token, "EX", 300)
    return product`}</code></pre>

<p>Three details make it correct. The lease must be taken before the database read, and the read must really start after it, not inside a transaction or snapshot the request opened earlier; then any change committed after the read is followed by a delete that removes the lease. The token must be unique to this reader, or one reader's fill could consume another's lease. And the placeholder needs a short expiry, so a crashed reader does not block the key: a little longer than a slow fill, not generous, because while it lives every other reader of that key goes to the database. A fill that arrives after the lease expired is refused, which is safe.</p>

<p><code>SET … IFEQ</code> compares the whole current value with the one you give. It does not create a missing key, and like any <code>SET</code> it replaces the key's expiry: without an expiry option the value would never expire, and with <code>KEEPTTL</code> it would keep the lease's two seconds. So always pass <code>EX</code>. Its companion <code>DELEX</code> deletes a key only if it holds a given value, so a reader whose database read fails can release its lease at once. Before Redis 8.4, the conditional fill is a short Lua script; <ArticleRef slug="clients-and-pipelining">Redis clients: round trips, pipelining and atomic operations</ArticleRef> covers scripts and other ways of making several steps atomic.</p>

<p>The lease has a second use. Every other reader that misses finds the placeholder and knows someone is already loading the key. In the code above they go to the database without filling. They could instead wait briefly and read again, which is what Facebook does: its servers hand out a token for a key at most once every 10 seconds and tell other clients to wait. On keys that are both heavily read and often written, which the paper calls a thundering herd, that cut the peak database query rate from 17,000 a second to 1,300. <ArticleRef slug="stampedes">Cache stampedes and the thundering herd</ArticleRef> builds on this lease.</p>

<p>A lease fixes a fill that races a delete, and nothing else. It does nothing if the delete is never sent, nothing for writers that update the cache instead of deleting, and nothing for a fill that reads a lagging replica after the delete, because no later delete comes to cancel it.</p>

<h2 id="versions">Out-of-order writers and versioned values</h2>

<p><ArticleRef slug="caching-patterns">Caching patterns</ArticleRef> described write-through, where the writer updates the cached copy after committing instead of deleting it, and said that two writers can leave the older value cached. Figure 6 shows how, with the shop's price changing twice in quick succession. The version shown beside each price is a number the database increments every time the row changes.</p>

<VersionFigure number={6} />

<p>With plain <code>SET</code>s, the cache keeps whichever write arrived last, and that was the older one. Deleting is immune, because a delete carries no value: two deletes in either order leave the key absent. That is why Facebook's paper and <ArticleRef slug="caching-patterns">Caching patterns</ArticleRef> both prefer delete.</p>

<p>When you do want to write values, for write-through or because an event stream delivers them, let each value carry the version the database gave it and keep a value only if it is newer than what is cached. Choose "if newer" in Figure 6 and writer 1's late write is refused. A duplicated event is refused the same way.</p>

<p>Where does the version come from? The safest source is the database: a version column incremented in the same <code>UPDATE</code> that changes the row and returned by it, or the position of the change in the database's log. A timestamp taken by the application is weaker, because two servers' clocks disagree and two changes can share a millisecond.</p>

<p>Now choose the third option. The check compares the incoming version with what Redis holds, so it only works while Redis still holds the newer one. If the key is evicted or deleted between the two writes, the late version 8 is compared with nothing and accepted. Meta's engineers describe precisely this case: if the entry with the newer version is evicted before the older fill arrives, the cache loses knowledge of the newer data.</p>

<p>The partial fix is not to delete. A writer that invalidates with versions writes a <strong>tombstone</strong> instead: an entry holding the new version and no value, with a TTL. Readers treat a tombstone as a miss, and a fill of the same version is allowed to replace it. The comparison has to happen inside Redis, in one step, or another write could slip in between the check and the set. This short script does it, storing each entry as a hash with a version field and, unless it is a tombstone, a value field:</p>

<pre><code>{`-- KEYS[1]: the cache key. ARGV: version, value ("" for a tombstone), TTL in seconds.
-- Lua numbers are doubles: keep versions below 2^53.
local cur = tonumber(redis.call("HGET", KEYS[1], "version") or "-1")
local new = tonumber(ARGV[1])
local tombstone = redis.call("HEXISTS", KEYS[1], "value") == 0
-- Accept a newer version, or a value of the same version over a tombstone.
if new > cur or (new == cur and tombstone and ARGV[2] ~= "") then
  redis.call("DEL", KEYS[1])
  if ARGV[2] == "" then redis.call("HSET", KEYS[1], "version", ARGV[1])
  else redis.call("HSET", KEYS[1], "version", ARGV[1], "value", ARGV[2]) end
  redis.call("EXPIRE", KEYS[1], ARGV[3])
  return 1
end
return 0`}</code></pre>

<p>Choose the fourth option in Figure 6 to see what the tombstone buys. It is Figure 2's slow reader again: its version-7 fill arrives after the writer's version-8 tombstone and is refused, and the next reader's version-8 fill replaces the tombstone. So with tombstones, version checks close the stale-set race too. They also do something a lease cannot: a fill that reads version 7 from a lagging replica after the change is refused as well, while the tombstone lives, so give it a TTL longer than your worst replication lag. Eviction can still remove a tombstone, so the key's TTL remains the safety net; <ArticleRef slug="expiration-and-eviction">Cache expiration and eviction</ArticleRef> explains which keys an eviction policy chooses.</p>

<p>On Redis 8.4 and later, with the version kept inside the value, the check can be a loop instead of a script: read, compare versions, write with <code>SET … IFEQ</code> (or <code>SET … NX</code> if the key was missing), and retry if the write is refused.</p>

<h2 id="lost-deletes">Lost deletes: transactional outbox and change data capture</h2>

<p>Every remedy so far assumes the writer's delete is sent. Figure 1 showed what happens when it is not: the copy is stale until it expires. The commit and the delete are writes to two systems, and nothing makes them one transaction, so a deploy, an out-of-memory kill or a timeout between the two steps loses the delete. This is the <strong>dual-write problem</strong>, and retrying in the same process does not solve it, because the process may be what died.</p>

<p>The fix is to make the instruction to delete part of the database transaction. In the <strong>transactional outbox</strong> pattern, the writer inserts a row saying "invalidate product:42" into an outbox table in the same transaction that changes the price, so both commit or neither does. A separate relay reads new outbox rows, sends the deletes and marks the rows done. Figure 7 shows the difference when the writer dies straight after committing.</p>

<OutboxFigure number={7} />

<p>The relay can fail too. If it dies after deleting but before marking the row done, it deletes again when it restarts. Relays and brokers generally deliver at least once, so duplicates are normal, and a delete is idempotent: doing it twice leaves the same state as doing it once. That is another reason to send deletes rather than values, which would need the version check from the previous section.</p>

<p>A variant reads the database's own change log instead of an outbox table, a technique called <strong>change data capture</strong>. Debezium, a widely used tool, publishes each change committed in PostgreSQL, MySQL and other databases as an event, at least once. A consumer turns each change to a product row into a <code>DEL</code> of the keys built from it. Facebook invalidates memcache this way, and notes the benefit: deletes that come from what the database committed can be replayed if any are lost.</p>

<p>Redis Pub/Sub is not reliable enough for this. It delivers each message at most once: on Redis 8.10, <code>PUBLISH invalidate product:42</code> with no subscriber connected returned 0, and the message was gone. Keyspace notifications are built on Pub/Sub. Use a durable log: an outbox table, a Kafka topic fed by change data capture, or a Redis stream on an instance with persistence and no eviction, not on the cache itself.</p>

<p>The cost brings us back to Figure 1. The delete now travels through a log and a relay, so the gap between commit and delete grows from a millisecond to tens or hundreds of milliseconds. Many teams do both: the writer deletes immediately, keeping the usual gap small, and the change stream deletes again, catching the deletes the writer lost and acting as a delayed double delete for free.</p>

<p>There is one more way to lose a delete, and the outbox does not catch it. Redis replicates asynchronously, so when a primary fails, the replica promoted in its place may lack the latest writes, even ones Redis had acknowledged. A <code>DEL</code> can vanish this way after the relay has marked its row done. So can the delete that cancels a lease, a tombstone, or a generation increment (described below), each letting old data back in. A restart from an older snapshot does the same. The TTL is the bound that still holds. <code>WAIT 1 100</code> after an important invalidation waits up to 100 ms for a replica to acknowledge it, which makes the loss less likely, but the Redis documentation says even a replicated write can still be lost in a failover. The practical remedy is the log you already have: after a failover, replay the last few minutes of invalidations. Deletes are idempotent, so replaying too many is harmless. <ArticleRef slug="replication-and-persistence">Redis replication, persistence and failover</ArticleRef> explains what a failover can lose.</p>

<h2 id="fan-out">Invalidation fan-out: tag sets and generation numbers</h2>

<p>So far one change meant one key. <ArticleRef slug="keys-and-values">Cache keys and cached values</ArticleRef> showed that caching larger results, such as listing pages, saves more work per hit but means one change touches many entries. When the kettle's price changes, every cached page that shows it is wrong, and the writer cannot easily name them all.</p>

<p>There are three common answers. The first is a <strong>tag set</strong>: whenever the application caches a page, it also adds the page's key, with <code>SADD</code>, to a set named after each thing the page depends on, such as <code>tag:product:42</code>. To invalidate, it deletes every key in the set, and the set. The second is a <strong>generation number</strong>: a counter such as <code>gen:category:7</code> that is part of every key built from that category, as in <code>category:7:g3:cheap:p1</code>. To invalidate, <code>INCR</code> the counter; keys built from the old generation are never looked up again. Figure 8 runs both on a category of twelve cached pages, three of which show the kettle.</p>

<FanoutFigure number={8} />

<p>With the tag set, watch a change flash through the three pages that show the kettle: they are deleted, the other nine stay cached, and Redis holds only pages that can be served. The cost is work: a delete per tagged page, and a second command on every fill. Switch to the generation number. A change is one <code>INCR</code>, however many pages there are, but it discards every page of the category, including the nine that never showed the kettle. The old pages move to the unreachable row and occupy memory until their TTL ends. So for every page it can serve, Redis holds about TTL ÷ time between changes unreachable copies: about three here, with a 20-second TTL and a change every 7 seconds, but about sixty for a one-hour TTL on a category that changes every minute.</p>

<p>A generation also costs a second round trip on every read: the page's key contains the generation, so the client cannot name the page until it has read the counter. One short script can read both, or the application can cache the generation for a second or two, adding that much staleness. In return, a generation is immune to the stale-set race, as long as fills read the primary: a fill that read old data read the old generation first, so it lands on a key nobody will look up. A fill that reads the new generation and then a lagging replica still stores old data under the new key.</p>

<p>Each scheme has a trap. The tag set's is a race of its own. If the invalidator reads the members and deletes them in a second command, a reader can add a new page in between; the delete then removes the set holding that page's name, and the page stays cached with nothing left to find it. So invalidate with one short script that reads the members and unlinks them and the set; nothing runs in between. On the fill side, run <code>SADD</code> before <code>SET</code>: a reader that dies between them leaves a harmless name without a page, not a page without a name. Give the set a TTL at least as long as its pages', set in the same script or <code>MULTI</code> as the <code>SADD</code> so a reader that dies cannot leave it with none. If every page has the same TTL, a plain <code>EXPIRE</code> does. If TTLs differ, use <code>EXPIRE … NX</code>, which gives a new set its first TTL, then <code>EXPIRE … GT</code>, which only lengthens it; <code>GT</code> alone does nothing to a set with no TTL, so the set would never expire. And use <code>UNLINK</code> for large sets: on a laptop, <code>DEL</code> of a million names took between tens and hundreds of milliseconds across runs, during which <ArticleRef slug="how-redis-works">Redis serves nobody else</ArticleRef>, while <code>UNLINK</code> returned at once and freed the memory in a background thread.</p>

<p>The generation number's trap is losing the counter. If <code>gen:category:7</code> is evicted and the application treats a missing counter as 1, any generation-1 pages still in memory are served again. Start a missing counter from a value no earlier generation can have used, such as Redis's own clock (<code>TIME</code>) in microseconds, written with <code>SET … NX</code> so concurrent readers agree; no counter is incremented a million times a second, so the clock is always ahead of it. Or keep counters, like the markers below, in a Redis that does not evict.</p>

<p>The third answer needs no invalidation at all: put the data's own version in the key, so an updated record simply has a new key. Rails does this with <code>cache_key_with_version</code>. Versioned keys suit one record or a tree of records, but cannot express a new product joining a category without something like a generation number.</p>

<p>So tag sets pay in work at invalidation time; generations pay in memory, discarded pages and a round trip per read; versioned keys pay in memory too. If invalidations are frequent and pages many, generations are usually cheaper; if memory is tight or pages are large, tags are. In Redis Cluster, a command or script that touches several keys, such as the tag script or one that reads a counter and a page, needs them in one hash slot; <ArticleRef slug="cluster-and-hot-keys">Redis Cluster, sharding and hot keys</ArticleRef> explains.</p>

<h2 id="read-your-writes">Read-your-writes for the person who made the change</h2>

<p>One reader matters more than the rest: the person who made the change. Most users will never notice a price that is half a second old, but the merchant who saved £35 and sees £39 on reload will assume the save failed. Figure 9 shows the problem where users meet it most often, on their own profile: Sam renames themselves Samira, and invalidations reach Redis through a relay half a second after the commit.</p>

<MarkerFigure number={9} />

<p>There are three ways to keep the promise for the writer without making everyone else pay.</p>

<p>The first is to delete from the writer's own request before it responds, even if a change stream also deletes. Facebook's web servers do this for their own cluster, to give one user's request read-after-write behaviour. It works when the delay is in the invalidation, but not when the user's next read misses and refills from a replica that has not caught up.</p>

<p>The second is a <strong>recent-write marker</strong>, the choice in Figure 9. After the write, the application sets a short-lived key for that user, such as <code>SET recent-write:user:7 1 EX 10</code>. While it exists, that user's reads skip the cache and go to the primary database. Its lifetime must exceed the worst invalidation delay or replication lag you expect. A marker differs from cached data: losing a cached value costs a miss, but losing the marker silently breaks the promise. Keep markers in a Redis that does not evict them, or accept an occasional stale read; <ArticleRef slug="expiration-and-eviction">Cache expiration and eviction</ArticleRef> describes a separate instance for data that must not be evicted.</p>

<p>The third keeps the state with the user. After a write, return the row's new version to the client, in a cookie or the response; on the next read, treat a cached value with an older version as a miss and read the primary. This survives eviction, but requires versioned values. If the client keeps the highest version it has seen, from reads as well as writes, the same check also gives that user monotonic reads of those keys. The client carries a version per key, so this suits a few keys, such as the user's own profile.</p>

<h2 id="choosing">Choosing what to use</h2>

<p>Most applications need only the first two rows of this table. The rest are for specific kinds of data, chosen by what goes wrong and what it costs.</p>

<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Invalidation techniques, what they fix and what they cost">
<table>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Technique</th><th scope="col">What it fixes</th><th scope="col">What it costs</th><th scope="col">Redis commands</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><th scope="row">TTL on every key</th><td>Caps every failure: lost deletes, races, failovers, bugs</td><td>A miss per expiry; does not lower the average staleness from races</td><td><code>SET … EX</code></td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Commit, then delete</th><td>Normal invalidation</td><td>One miss per write</td><td><code>DEL</code>, or <code>UNLINK</code> for many keys</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Delayed double delete</th><td>Racing fills that land before the second delete</td><td>A durable delayed job; misses slow fills and lagging replicas</td><td><code>DEL</code> twice</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Lease</th><td>A fill that races a delete; also many readers filling one key</td><td>One more command per miss; readers must recognise placeholders</td><td><code>SET … NX PX</code>, then <code>SET … IFEQ</code> (8.4+) or a script</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Version check and tombstones</th><td>Writers out of order, duplicates, and, while a tombstone lives, racing fills, including from a lagging replica</td><td>A version from the database; a script or retry loop; eviction forgets versions</td><td><code>EVAL</code>, or <code>GET</code> then <code>SET … IFEQ</code> or <code>SET … NX</code> (8.4+)</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Outbox or change data capture</th><td>Deletes lost between commit and delete; with a replay, deletes lost in a Redis failover</td><td>A relay to run; a longer gap before the delete</td><td><code>DEL</code> (idempotent, so duplicates are harmless)</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Tag set</th><td>One change affecting many keys, deleted exactly</td><td>Work in proportion to the keys; a set to keep in step</td><td><code>SADD</code> before <code>SET</code>; a script to <code>UNLINK</code> members and set</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Generation number</th><td>One change affecting many keys, in one command</td><td>Old keys in memory; unchanged pages discarded; a second round trip per read</td><td><code>INCR</code>, <code>GET</code> (<code>MGET</code> for several counters), <code>SET … NX</code></td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Recent-write marker, or a version carried by the client</th><td>The writer seeing their own change; a carried version also stops a user going backwards</td><td>Database reads for that user for a few seconds</td><td><code>SET … EX</code></td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
</div>

<p>Start with how long a wrong value may be shown, and give every key that TTL: it is the only remedy that caps every failure at once, including ones you have not thought of. Then estimate writes a second × mean fill time; if that share matters, add leases for delete-based caches or version checks for caches written with values. Three points the table cannot make:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Replicas change the picture.</strong> If fills read replicas, races follow most writes and leases do not help: keep fills of changing keys on the primary, use tombstones that outlive the lag, or delete only once the replicas have the change, as Facebook does. Fills on the primary remove the replica cause of a user seeing an older value after a newer one, but not the others, such as a stale set or a lost delete; only a version the client carries rules them all out, for the keys it carries.</li>
  <li><strong>A change log needs a replay.</strong> After a Redis failover, replay the last few minutes of invalidations from your outbox or change log.</li>
  <li><strong>The writer is a special reader.</strong> Give the person who just made a change their own read path, rather than tightening consistency for everyone.</li>
</ul>

<p>One part of the lease is left for later: what happens when hundreds of readers miss the same key at once, after a delete, an expiry or a restart. The lease sent all but one of them elsewhere; where they go, and how to keep them from overloading the database, is the subject of <ArticleRef slug="stampedes">Cache stampedes and the thundering herd</ArticleRef>.</p>

